# Depth pass D: plain-language versions of each summary section (index -> text)
PLAIN = {
 0: "Look around a pool hall: every cue and ball is one particular object, but we describe them all with general words like “round,” “red,” and “triangular.” Those general features — philosophers call them universals — are shared by many things at once. You can know facts about “triangle-ness” (it always has three sides), yet you can’t pick it up or see it on its own. That makes it a strange but important kind of thing, and the whole chapter is about what kind.",
 1: "Three more things behave the same way. A statement’s meaning (“snow is white”) is the same whether you say it in English or German, and erasing every written copy wouldn’t make it false. Numbers aren’t the ink marks we write for them. And “ways things could have been” — like Mars having three moons — are things we can think about carefully but never visit.",
 2: "So the big question is: what kind of existence do these things have? One answer says they don’t really exist (there are only words); another says they exist, but only because human minds make them up. Feser defends a third answer, “realism”: they are real, not physical, and not human inventions. At this stage he isn’t yet saying where they exist.",
 3: "Feser gives six reasons for realism. Triangle-ness doesn’t disappear if every triangle is destroyed; math truths were true before people existed and can’t be changed by us; there are infinitely many numbers but only finitely many things and thoughts; some truths would hold even if nothing existed; science relies on general laws and math; and many possibilities have nothing to do with us. All of these show independence from the physical world and from human minds — not yet from every mind.",
 4: "Some people say there’s no “redness,” only the word “red.” But why use the word for exactly these things? Saying “because they resemble each other” just brings in another shared feature — resemblance — and the problem repeats forever. Worse, the word “red” is itself one thing used by many people; if every use were a different word, nobody could ever communicate.",
 5: "Others say these things exist, but only as ideas we humans construct. The trouble is that you and I can think the very same thought, which private inventions couldn’t allow — we couldn’t even communicate. And if logic were just a product of evolution or culture, the argument for that claim would itself be just a product of evolution or culture, so it defeats itself.",
 6: "Plato said these things live in a separate, non-physical, non-mental realm. Feser argues that such things couldn’t do anything, so we couldn’t know about them and they couldn’t explain why real triangles are triangular. Also, “animal in general” is neither human nor non-human, but no real individual thing can be neither — so it can’t exist as a thing by itself. And real trees would become mere copies of the “true” tree elsewhere, which is absurd.",
 7: "Aristotle’s alternative: general features exist inside real things (the “animal-ness” in your dog), and exist as general, abstract ideas only when a mind thinks about them by setting the individual details aside. So nothing is abstract unless some mind is doing the abstracting. This view also explains many facts about what things can and can’t do, based on their natures.",
 8: "But Aristotle’s view, if it stops with human minds, can’t handle some cases. If nothing had ever existed, it would still have been possible for a world to exist — yet there’d be no things or human minds to ground that. Unicorns are possible though none exist and nobody had to imagine them first. And math and logic would be true with no humans around at all.",
 9: "Feser’s answer is “Scholastic realism,” named for the medieval school tradition: keep Aristotle’s view that these things exist only in things or in minds, but add that there must also be a mind beyond all human minds that holds them — an eternal, infinite, divine mind. This idea goes back to Saint Augustine and was later defended by Leibniz. By ruling out the other options, Feser concludes such a mind exists.",
 10: "Why must that mind be special? Human minds come and go, so truths that are true no matter what can’t depend on them. The same goes for aliens, and even for angels, who never die but still exist only because they were created. So the ultimate home of these truths has to be a mind that could not possibly fail to exist.",
 11: "Could there be several such minds splitting the work? No: every truth is connected to every other by logic (one follows from another, or they conflict, or they don’t), so any mind holding some of them must hold the connections to all the rest. And since there are infinitely many truths, this one mind knows all the general truths and possibilities there are.",
 12: "Finally, a mind that couldn’t fail to exist can’t depend on anything outside itself or have any unfulfilled capacity. From the earlier chapter, such a being must be one, all-powerful, perfectly good, unchanging, non-physical, and timeless. And it must know every truth, including everyday facts, because not knowing something would be an unfulfilled capacity. That is what people mean by God.",
 13: "The philosopher Greg Welty offers six tests for any theory of these abstract things: they must be real apart from us, necessary, able to “represent” things the way thoughts do, able to explain what’s possible, plentiful enough, and not needlessly complicated. Only the view that they are ideas in God’s mind passes all six. For example, a drawing of Socrates as a carpenter doesn’t make it possible for him to have been one — but a plan in the mind of someone able to make it happen does.",
 14: "Feser then sets out the reasoning in 29 short steps. First he rules out the views that deny these things are real or say we made them up. Then he rules out Plato’s separate realm and the “only human minds” view. Finally he shows the remaining mind must be one, all-knowing, and have all of God’s traditional attributes.",
 15: "Some critics say that if these things are thoughts in God’s mind, they aren’t “abstract” anymore. Feser replies that philosophers don’t agree on what “abstract” means — and on his definition, something is abstract precisely because a mind has abstracted it. So the objection just assumes a definition he rejects.",
 16: "Another critic, Brian Leftow, says that if God’s nature makes “water is H₂O” necessary, then God’s existence would depend on facts about water. Feser answers that arithmetic makes “two rocks plus two rocks is four rocks” necessary, yet arithmetic doesn’t depend on rocks. Logical connection is not the same as dependence.",
 17: "A mathematical objection says there can’t be a complete “collection” of all truths, so nobody could know them all. Feser gives three replies: the same reasoning would stop us from saying anything about “all statements,” which we obviously can; knowing everything doesn’t require a collection, only that no truth is left out; and God’s knowledge isn’t a pile of separate items anyway.",
 18: "One critic, himself a believer, says the argument cheats by assuming what it wants to prove. Feser replies that even atheists who follow Plato agree that possibilities are eternal, so the starting point doesn’t assume God. The argument starts from shared ground and then argues that a divine mind explains it better than Plato’s realm does.",
}
